You're right. I did some tests and log-rotate refuses to rotate a symlink f=
ile

# ls -l /var/log/nginx/*
lrwxrwxrwx 1 www-data adm 16 Jan 13 18:56 /var/log/nginx/error.log -> /etc/=
ld.so.conf

# logrotate --verbose /etc/logrotate.d/nginx=20
reading config file /etc/logrotate.d/nginx
Handling 1 logs
rotating pattern: /var/log/nginx/*.log  after 1 days (52 rotations)
empty log files are not rotated, old logs are removed
considering log /var/log/nginx/error.log
  log /var/log/nginx/error.log is symbolic link. Rotation of symbolic links=
 is not allowed to avoid security issues -- skipping.
not running prerotate script, since no logs will be rotated
not running postrotate script, since no logs were rotated


So the issue is than when in var/log/nginx/ there are standard logs (non sy=
mlinked)
that need to be rotated (appart from the malicious symlinked one), then log=
rotate
will rotate those ones, finally running the post-rotate script that send SI=
GURSR1
to the nginx pid.

Then nginx upon USR1 receive does the wrong thing... https://trac.nginx.org=
/nginx/ticket/376
=20

/me happy to know that logrotate has a sane behaviour and avoids trying to =
rotate symlinks.


